systems=[
 ('02-M01','Explain how greater demand can direct resources towards baskets in a market system. Assume the higher demand permits a higher selling price, costs are initially unchanged and extra inputs are available.','Higher demand permits a higher price, increasing expected profitability while costs are unchanged. Producers have an incentive to expand output, so they obtain more materials or labour, directing resources towards baskets.',['Starting change (1): increased demand/higher price. Mechanism/because (1): greater expected profitability with costs unchanged. Consequence (1): incentive to expand production. Resource allocation (1): obtain additional materials or labour. Total 4.'], 'Explain why an invented workshop hires extra weaving time after higher basket demand raises its expected profit, with labour available.','The stronger expected reward makes expanded basket production worthwhile, so labour is directed towards it.',['Context (1): higher expected profit in basket production. Reason/consequence (1): incentive directs available labour towards the activity.']),
 ('02-M02','Explain how a central plan can direct resources towards water provision rather than recreation. Both projects need the same scarce construction team.','The authority prioritises water access, assigns the team and equipment to it, and instructs the responsible producers. If implemented, the assignment increases resources used for water provision while forgoing the competing recreation project.',['Starting change (1): water chosen as a priority. Mechanism (1): authority assigns team/equipment. Because (1): producers implement the allocation instructions. Consequence (1): resources serve water instead of the alternative. Total 4.'],'Explain how a planned system allocates a construction team when an authority instructs it to build a water point instead of a recreation site.','The authority’s priority and assignment direct the team to water provision, displacing its competing use at the recreation site.',['Context (1): authority’s allocation instruction. Reason/conclusion (1): team serves water rather than its alternative use.']),
 ('02-M03','Explain resource allocation in a mixed system where private shops sell water containers and government finances basic water points for low-income households.','Private shops allocate inputs in response to purchases and expected profit. Public finance directs labour and equipment towards water access that low-income purchasing power may not support privately. Both market signals and government priorities therefore affect resource use.',['Initial change/allocation (1): private shops respond to paying demand. Because/mechanism (1): expected profit guides their input use. Public mechanism (1): finance directs resources to basic water points. Consequence (1): private allocation and public access priorities coexist. Total 4.'],'Explain why private sales of containers alongside publicly financed water points illustrate a mixed system.','Private purchases guide the shops’ decisions while public spending guides water-point provision; both mechanisms influence allocation.',['Context (1): private container sales and public water points. Reason/conclusion (1): both market and government decisions allocate resources.']),
]
