context='Invented teaching data: a hypothetical district has households needing basic water access but unable to cover a private service’s full price. Private suppliers can respond quickly to profitable demand. The authority can finance a basic service but has limited maintenance capacity. The objective is reliable access while avoiding wasted resources.'
evalanswer='Public finance can improve access for households whose purchasing power does not support the private service. However, limited maintenance capacity could make the service unreliable and waste resources. Private incentives can encourage rapid response where customers can pay. A mixed arrangement is justified if public access support is targeted and maintenance can be secured; public funding alone does not establish reliable delivery.'
