t.card(p,'01 02 03','Invented hypothetical district: N$6,000 and one construction team can provide either a basic water point or a recreation site. Low-income households need water but cannot cover the full private service price. Describe three features of the allocation problem (6). Explain how market and planned mechanisms could allocate the scarce resources differently (7). Discuss whether combining private supply with public access support best meets the objective of reliable essential access, given limited public maintenance capacity (7).','The funds are limited, the team has competing uses, and selecting one project forgoes the other. Market suppliers respond to expected paying demand and profit, so low purchasing power may limit the water service despite need. A planning authority can prioritise water and assign the budget and team to it. Both mechanisms still face the same scarce inputs and opportunity cost. Public support can improve access while retaining private responsiveness, but maintenance constraints may undermine reliability. Prefer a mixed arrangement only if access support and ongoing maintenance are feasible; otherwise the funding decision alone cannot establish the desired result.',['Context/data description (6): limited budget (1) with N$6,000 stated (1); scarce team (1) needed by both projects (1); choice between projects (1) forgoes the alternative benefit (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): market follows paying demand (1), profit incentives (1), low purchasing power limits demand-backed provision (1); authority sets water priority (1), assigns budget/team (1), implementers direct resources accordingly (1); scarcity/opportunity cost remains in either system (1). Both sides/judgement (7): public support can improve access (1) for low-income households (1); private responsiveness remains useful (1); weak maintenance threatens reliability (1) and resource use (1); support a mixed arrangement (1) conditional on workable maintenance (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'])
