t.card(p,'03','Evaluate two allocation arrangements in separate seven-mark plans. Invented Case 1: low-income households need basic water, private suppliers require full cost recovery, and an authority can fund a service but has limited maintenance capacity. The priority is reliable basic access. Case 2: private producers compete to supply non-essential household goods; customers can compare offers and pay, demand changes frequently, and the authority’s information about those changes is slow. The priority is responsiveness with limited waste. Include opposing arguments, a decisive condition and a judgement for each.','Case 1: public access support addresses the gap between need and paying demand, but inadequate maintenance can make it unreliable. A mixed arrangement is justified if maintenance and finance can be sustained. Case 2: market incentives and customer comparison can encourage producers to respond to changing wants; nevertheless information or delivery problems can still occur. Prefer market-led allocation for the stated responsiveness objective if competition and reliable information remain meaningful, with intervention targeted at an identified problem rather than assumed necessary everywhere.',['Case 1 allocation (7): argument for public support reaches low-income users (1), serving essential access (1); argument against maintenance gaps (1) threaten reliable provision (1); condition is sustained maintenance/finance (1); judgement supports a mixed arrangement (1) because it meets access under that condition (1). Case 2 marks (7): argument for profit signals reward response (1), customer comparison informs choice (1); argument against producer information can be incomplete (1) and delivery can lag (1); condition is meaningful choice and reliable information (1); judgement favours market-led allocation (1) for the stated responsiveness objective under that condition (1). Total 14.'])
